=== TOPIC ===
id: aiml-ml-pipeline
title: Machine Learning Pipeline Sub-Roadmap
roadmapId: ai-ml-engineer
type: subroadmap
targetSubroadmap: machine-learning
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 110
summary: End-to-end data pipeline from raw ingestion and feature engineering to cross-validation and hyperparameter tuning.
explanation: Production machine learning requires a repeatable pipeline architecture: ingestion, validation, cleaning, feature scaling, encoding, model training, cross-validation, and artifact serialization. Pipelines eliminate data leakage between train and test distributions.
codeExample: # Conceptual scikit-learn Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\npipe = Pipeline([\n    ('scaler', StandardScaler()),\n    ('classifier', LogisticRegression())\n])\nprint("Pipeline defined:", pipe)
objective: Construct leak-free preprocessing pipelines using scikit-learn transformers
objective: Apply k-fold cross-validation and stratified sampling
objective: Tune hyperparameters systematically using grid search and Bayesian optimization
pitfall: Data leakage: fitting scalers or encoders on the entire dataset prior to train/test split
pitfall: Evaluating models on training data leading to unspotted overfitting
prerequisites: aiml-dsa-subroadmap
resource: Made With ML — MLOps|https://madewithml.com/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aiml-supervised-learning
title: Supervised Learning: Regression, Classification & Ensembles
roadmapId: ai-ml-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Linear regression, logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, and gradient boosted trees (XGBoost).
explanation: Supervised learning trains models on labeled datasets (features X mapping to target y). Linear and logistic models establish baseline relationships. Tree-based ensemble methods (Random Forest bagging, XGBoost/LightGBM gradient boosting) represent state-of-the-art architectures for tabular predictive tasks.
codeExample: # XGBoost Training Pattern\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nX_train = np.random.randn(100, 4)\ny_train = (X_train[:, 0] > 0).astype(int)\n\nclf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=10)\nclf.fit(X_train, y_train)\nprint("Predictions:", clf.predict(X_train[:3]))
objective: Train and evaluate linear and logistic regression models with regularization (L1/L2)
objective: Explain the bias-variance tradeoff and ensemble voting/boosting principles
objective: Train high-accuracy gradient boosted decision trees using XGBoost
pitfall: Overfitting trees by allowing unlimited depth on small datasets
pitfall: Failing to handle class imbalance resulting in misleading high accuracy scores
pitfall: Neglecting feature scaling for distance-based or linear models
prerequisites: aiml-ml-pipeline
resource: scikit-learn User Guide|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html|documentation
