summary: Translate a business or scientific question into a measurable machine-learning task.
explanation: Define the prediction target, unit of analysis, decision context, baseline, and success metric before choosing an algorithm. Identify whether the task is classification, regression, ranking, clustering, or forecasting, and check whether a model is appropriate at all.
codeExample: 
objective: State the target and intended decision
objective: Choose a baseline and task-appropriate metric
objective: Identify leakage and data-availability risks
pitfall: Starting with a model before defining the target
pitfall: Using a metric unrelated to the real decision
prerequisites:
resource: scikit-learn User Guide|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-data-preparation
title: Data Preparation and Feature Engineering
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Create reliable training data and features without leaking information from evaluation data.
explanation: Inspect missingness, label quality, class imbalance, data types, and sampling. Split data before fitting preprocessing steps; use pipelines so transformations are learned only from training folds. Engineer features from information available at prediction time.
codeExample: from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression

model = make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(), StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression())
model.fit(X_train, y_train)  # fit preprocessing only on training data
objective: Build a reproducible preprocessing pipeline
objective: Select an appropriate split strategy
objective: Explain leakage from future or target-derived features
pitfall: Fitting scalers or imputers before splitting
pitfall: Randomly splitting time-dependent or grouped records
prerequisites: mleng-problem-framing
resource: scikit-learn User Guide|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-model-training
title: Model Training and Regularization
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Train candidate models while controlling overfitting and tracking reproducible experiments.
explanation: Establish a simple baseline, then compare suitable models using a consistent validation procedure. Regularization, early stopping, cross-validation, and hyperparameter search help manage generalization. Save seeds, data versions, parameters, metrics, and artifacts needed to reproduce results.
codeExample: 
objective: Compare a baseline against a candidate model
objective: Use cross-validation or a valid holdout strategy
objective: Record experiment configuration and results
pitfall: Tuning repeatedly on the final test set
pitfall: Comparing models on different data splits
prerequisites: mleng-data-preparation
resource: scikit-learn User Guide|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-evaluation
title: Model Evaluation and Error Analysis
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Evaluate model quality, calibration, subgroup performance, and failure modes.
explanation: Report metrics with a confusion matrix or task-specific error analysis. For imbalanced classes, accuracy alone can be misleading. Consider calibration, confidence intervals, subgroup behavior, and the operational cost of false positives and false negatives.
codeExample: from sklearn.metrics import classification_report

y_pred = model.predict(X_test)
print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred))
objective: Choose metrics that match error costs
objective: Inspect representative false predictions
objective: Evaluate relevant data slices and uncertainty
pitfall: Reporting only a single aggregate score
pitfall: Treating validation performance as a guarantee of production performance
prerequisites: mleng-model-training
resource: scikit-learn User Guide|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-serving
title: Model Serving and Inference Contracts
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Package a trained model behind a stable and testable inference interface.
explanation: A serving contract specifies input schema, output schema, preprocessing, model version, error handling, and latency expectations. Batch inference and online inference have different throughput and freshness requirements. Validate inputs and version the model with its preprocessing assets.
codeExample: from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI()
class Features(BaseModel):
    values: list[float]

@app.post("/predict")
def predict(data: Features):
    return {"prediction": model.predict([data.values]).tolist()}
objective: Define and validate an inference schema
objective: Distinguish batch from online serving
objective: Measure latency and throughput
pitfall: Training and serving preprocessing diverging
pitfall: Deploying a model without a rollback path
prerequisites: mleng-evaluation
resource: Hugging Face NLP Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: mleng-monitoring
title: Production Model Monitoring
roadmapId: machine-learning-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Monitor technical health and data/model behavior after deployment.
explanation: Track service latency, error rates, resource use, input distributions, delayed ground-truth metrics, and changes in important subgroups. A drift alert is a signal to investigate, not proof that model quality has declined; retraining should follow a validated process.
codeExample: 
objective: Separate service metrics from model-quality metrics
objective: Define alert thresholds and ownership
objective: Design a safe review and retraining trigger
pitfall: Retraining automatically on every drift alert
pitfall: Monitoring inputs without checking eventual outcomes
prerequisites: mleng-serving
resource: Made With ML — MLOps|https://madewithml.com/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-model-selection
title: Selecting Models for an AI Application
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 45
summary: Choose a model or hosted AI service based on task fit, privacy, latency, quality, and cost.
explanation: Compare candidate models using representative tasks and acceptance criteria. Distinguish model capability from application reliability: retrieval, validation, user experience, and fallback logic may matter as much as raw benchmark scores.
codeExample: 
objective: Write a model-selection scorecard
objective: Test representative and edge-case inputs
objective: Document privacy and data-retention constraints
pitfall: Selecting only by benchmark rank
pitfall: Sending sensitive data to a service without reviewing its terms
prerequisites:
resource: Hugging Face NLP Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-inference-api
title: Inference APIs and Application Boundaries
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Integrate model inference behind a maintainable application boundary.
explanation: Keep provider-specific calls behind an adapter, set timeouts, handle rate limits, validate response shapes, and distinguish retryable failures from permanent errors. Log request metadata without exposing secrets or unnecessary user content.
codeExample: 
objective: Build a provider adapter interface
objective: Handle timeouts, throttling, and malformed responses
objective: Test failure and fallback behavior
pitfall: Unbounded retries that multiply cost
pitfall: Tightly coupling application logic to one provider response format
prerequisites: aieng-model-selection
resource: Hugging Face NLP Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-structured-output
title: Structured Outputs and Validation
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Turn model responses into validated application data rather than trusting free-form text.
explanation: Use constrained output formats or schemas where supported, then validate the returned object in application code. Schema compliance does not guarantee factual correctness or safe values; business rules and authorization still belong in deterministic code.
codeExample: from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

class TicketSummary(BaseModel):
    category: str
    priority: int = Field(ge=1, le=5)
    rationale: str

# Validate model output before using it in application logic.
objective: Define a response schema
objective: Validate semantic and business constraints
objective: Handle refusal, truncation, and invalid output
pitfall: Treating valid JSON as necessarily correct
pitfall: Using model-generated fields to bypass authorization
prerequisites: aieng-inference-api
resource: Hugging Face NLP Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-tool-use
title: Tool Use and Controlled Actions
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Connect model reasoning to explicitly permitted tools while keeping execution under application control.
explanation: Expose narrow tool definitions, validate arguments, enforce user permissions, and require confirmation for consequential actions. Tool output is untrusted input. Separate proposing an action from executing it, and keep an audit trail for sensitive operations.
codeExample: 
objective: Define minimal tool schemas and permissions
objective: Validate tool arguments and outputs
objective: Gate high-impact actions behind confirmation
pitfall: Allowing arbitrary shell or database access
pitfall: Assuming a model-generated request is authorized
prerequisites: aieng-structured-output
resource: Hugging Face Agents Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/agents-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-evaluation
title: Evaluating AI Application Quality
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Build a repeatable evaluation set for correctness, usefulness, safety, and operational performance.
explanation: Use curated representative examples, expected behaviors, adversarial cases, and human review for ambiguous judgments. Automated model-based grading can help scale evaluation but requires calibration against human judgments and can share the same blind spots as the model under test.
codeExample: 
objective: Create a versioned evaluation dataset
objective: Track quality, latency, and cost together
objective: Review disagreements and regression cases
pitfall: Evaluating only with hand-picked happy paths
pitfall: Changing prompts without rerunning regression tests
prerequisites: aieng-tool-use
resource: Hugging Face NLP Course|https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: aieng-security-cost
title: AI Security, Privacy, and Cost Controls
roadmapId: ai-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Reduce prompt-injection, data exposure, and runaway inference cost risks.
explanation: Treat retrieved documents and tool results as untrusted content. Apply least privilege, data minimization, output handling, per-user quotas, token limits, and cost monitoring. Red-team testing should include indirect prompt injection and unauthorized data-access attempts.
codeExample: 
objective: Apply least privilege to tools and data
objective: Set quotas and maximum input/output sizes
objective: Test prompt-injection and sensitive-data cases
pitfall: Relying on a system prompt as the only security boundary
pitfall: Logging API keys or full sensitive prompts
prerequisites: aieng-evaluation
resource: OWASP Web Security Testing Guide|https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/|documentation
