id: net-dns-dhcp
title: DNS, DHCP, and Core Network Services
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 50
summary: Understand how hosts obtain network configuration and resolve names.
explanation: DHCP can provide addresses and related configuration; DNS maps names to records such as A, AAAA, and CNAME. These services are separate, and failures can look similar to application outages. Use tools such as nslookup or dig to isolate name resolution from connectivity.
codeExample: 
objective: Describe a DHCP lease lifecycle
objective: Inspect common DNS record types
objective: Troubleshoot name-resolution failures
pitfall: Changing DNS records without considering TTL and caching
pitfall: Treating DNS resolution as proof the destination is reachable
prerequisites: net-switching-routing
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: net-transport-troubleshooting
title: TCP, UDP, TLS, and Network Troubleshooting
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Use transport and application evidence to narrow connectivity failures.
explanation: TCP provides ordered reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagrams without built-in delivery guarantees. TLS protects a connection when correctly configured. Troubleshooting should distinguish DNS, routing, port reachability, handshake, certificate, and application-layer failures.
codeExample: curl -v --connect-timeout 5 https://example.com/
nslookup example.com

# Use packet capture only on networks and traffic you are authorized to inspect.
objective: Compare TCP and UDP use cases
objective: Interpret basic connection and TLS errors
objective: Use packet capture or socket tools carefully
pitfall: Assuming a port being open proves the application is healthy
pitfall: Disabling certificate checks as a permanent fix
prerequisites: net-dns-dhcp
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: net-firewalls-segmentation
title: Firewalls, Segmentation, and Zero Trust
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Reduce network exposure through explicit traffic policy and segmented trust boundaries.
explanation: Firewalls enforce traffic rules; segmentation limits lateral movement and scopes failure. A zero-trust approach verifies access based on identity and context rather than assuming that network location alone establishes trust. Document intended flows and test both allowed and denied paths.
codeExample: 
objective: Write least-privilege traffic rules
objective: Separate public, application, and data tiers
objective: Test denied as well as permitted flows
pitfall: Using broad any-any rules to resolve a connectivity issue
pitfall: Treating an internal network as inherently trusted
prerequisites: net-transport-troubleshooting
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: net-monitoring-automation
title: Network Monitoring and Automation
roadmapId: network-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Monitor network health and automate repeatable configuration tasks safely.
explanation: Monitor interface errors, packet loss, latency, routing changes, and service reachability. Configuration automation should use version control, validation, limited credentials, staged changes, and rollback. Avoid collecting sensitive traffic content unless there is a justified and authorized need.
codeExample: 
objective: Choose meaningful network health indicators
objective: Version and validate configuration changes
objective: Plan safe rollback for automation
pitfall: Pushing untested config to every device at once
pitfall: Collecting packet contents without authorization
prerequisites: net-firewalls-segmentation
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-process-memory
title: Processes, Virtual Memory, and Runtime
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Understand process isolation, virtual address spaces, and program execution at the OS boundary.
explanation: A process owns resources and a virtual address space; threads within a process share much of that state. Virtual memory separates logical addresses from physical memory and supports isolation, mapping, and demand paging. Exact APIs differ across operating systems.
codeExample: 
objective: Distinguish process and thread resources
objective: Explain virtual memory and page faults
objective: Inspect process state with OS tools
pitfall: Assuming virtual addresses are physical addresses
pitfall: Sharing mutable process state without synchronization
prerequisites:
resource: Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces|https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/|book
=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-pointers-ownership
title: Memory Ownership and Resource Lifetimes
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Manage memory and other resources with explicit ownership and lifetime rules.
explanation: In C, the programmer must define who allocates, owns, transfers, and frees each resource. Dangling references, double frees, leaks, and use-after-free errors arise when lifetime rules are unclear. Tools such as sanitizers can expose many memory errors during testing.
codeExample: #include <stdlib.h>

int *make_value(int value) {
    int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
    if (p == NULL) return NULL;
    *p = value;
    return p; /* caller owns the allocation and must free it */
}
objective: Document ownership for API parameters and returns
objective: Handle allocation failure and cleanup paths
objective: Use sanitizers to find memory errors
pitfall: Freeing the same allocation twice
pitfall: Returning pointers whose storage lifetime has ended
prerequisites: sysprog-process-memory
resource: Beej's Guide to C Programming|https://beej.us/guide/bgc/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-files-syscalls
title: System Calls, Files, and I/O
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Use operating-system interfaces for file descriptors, processes, and I/O safely.
explanation: System calls cross the user/kernel boundary to request OS services. Low-level I/O can return partial results or be interrupted; robust code checks return values and handles errors. File descriptors and handles have lifetimes and permissions.
codeExample: #include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

ssize_t n;
do {
    n = write(fd, buffer, length);
} while (n < 0 && errno == EINTR);
/* Production code must also handle partial writes and other errors. */
objective: Check partial reads and writes
objective: Handle errors and resource cleanup
objective: Distinguish buffered library I/O from system calls
pitfall: Assuming one read always returns all requested bytes
pitfall: Ignoring permissions and path-handling risks
prerequisites: sysprog-pointers-ownership
resource: Learn C — Interactive C Tutorial|https://www.learn-c.org/en/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-concurrency
title: Threads, Synchronization, and Concurrency
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Coordinate concurrent execution without data races or deadlocks.
explanation: Threads can improve responsiveness and throughput but introduce shared-state hazards. Mutexes, condition variables, atomics, and message passing have different semantics. Correctness depends on invariants, lock ordering, and ownership, not simply on adding locks.
codeExample: /* Shared state must be protected by a consistent synchronization rule. */
pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
shared_count += 1;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
objective: Identify shared mutable state
objective: Use synchronization primitives appropriately
objective: Explain race conditions and deadlock conditions
pitfall: Holding locks across unpredictable blocking operations
pitfall: Assuming atomicity of compound operations
prerequisites: sysprog-files-syscalls
resource: Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces|https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/|book
=== TOPIC ===
id: sysprog-debugging
title: Native Debugging and Performance Profiling
roadmapId: systems-programmer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Diagnose native crashes and performance bottlenecks using symbols, traces, and profilers.
explanation: A reproducible failure and a minimal test case are often more valuable than speculative fixes. Debuggers inspect execution state; profilers reveal where time or resources are spent. Benchmarks must reflect realistic workloads and account for measurement noise.
codeExample: 
objective: Capture a useful stack trace
objective: Use sanitizers and a profiler to test hypotheses
objective: Benchmark before and after a change
pitfall: Optimizing based on intuition alone
pitfall: Removing debug checks without identifying the root cause
prerequisites: sysprog-concurrency
resource: PostgreSQL Documentation — Tutorial|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html|tutorial
