=== TOPIC ===
id: emb-c-firmware
title: C Firmware Structure and Memory Constraints
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Organize firmware around initialization, main loops, interrupts, and bounded resource use.
explanation: Firmware often has no general-purpose process environment and may use statically allocated buffers. Initialization order, stack size, interrupt context, and memory budgets should be explicit. Dynamic allocation may be unsuitable in some real-time or safety-constrained designs.
codeExample: int main(void) {
    board_init();
    peripherals_init();
    for (;;) {
        poll_inputs();
        update_state();
        service_outputs();
    }
}
objective: Structure startup and main-loop responsibilities
objective: Estimate static RAM and stack usage
objective: Keep interrupt handlers bounded
pitfall: Calling blocking APIs from an interrupt handler without checking constraints
pitfall: Allocating unbounded memory in long-running firmware
prerequisites: emb-mcu-architecture
resource: Making Embedded Systems — Resources|https://interrupt.memfault.com/blog/|articles
=== TOPIC ===
id: emb-peripherals-buses
title: GPIO, Timers, UART, SPI, and I²C
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Interface firmware with external components using common peripheral and serial-bus patterns.
explanation: GPIO handles digital signals; timers support periodic events and measurement; UART, SPI, and I²C provide different serial communication trade-offs. Correct integration requires matching voltage levels, clocking, bus addresses, timing, and device-specific protocol details.
codeExample: /* Pseudocode: always check the HAL return code and use a timeout. */
status = i2c_read(device_address, register_address, buffer, length, timeout_ms);
if (status != OK) {
    record_bus_error(status);
}
objective: Choose an interface based on device requirements
objective: Read a peripheral timing diagram
objective: Handle communication errors and timeouts
pitfall: Assuming buses share the same electrical characteristics
pitfall: Ignoring pull-ups, clock rates, or voltage compatibility
prerequisites: emb-c-firmware
resource: Making Embedded Systems — Resources|https://interrupt.memfault.com/blog/|articles
