id: backend-development-curr-queues-and-idempotent-processing
title: Queues and Idempotent Processing
roadmapId: backend-development
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: At-least-once delivery, deduplication, retries, poison messages, dead-letter queues and outbox/inbox patterns
explanation: Backend engineering requires explicit contracts and failure semantics across APIs, databases, caches, identity systems, and dependent services. Queues and Idempotent Processing covers at-least-once delivery, deduplication, retries, poison messages, dead-letter queues and outbox/inbox patterns. Work through the core mechanisms, the assumptions they rely on, and the trade-offs that affect correctness, maintainability, and performance. Connect the topic to the surrounding system rather than treating its terminology as an end in itself. Practice: Create a compact implementation or worked example that uses the topic. Explain the design, test normal and boundary cases, and note one trade-off or limitation.
codeExample: 
objective: Explain the purpose and operating model of queues and idempotent processing, including At-least-once delivery, deduplication, retries.
objective: Apply queues and idempotent processing to a representative implementation or analysis task, including poison messages, dead-letter queues and outbox/inbox patterns.
objective: Evaluate correctness, edge cases, limitations, and relevant complexity or operational trade-offs for queues and idempotent processing.
pitfall: Assuming operations complete in a fixed order or exactly once; ignoring cancellation, retries, shared state, and partial failure.
