=== TOPIC ===
id: android-platform-components
title: Android Platform Architecture and App Components
roadmapId: android-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Understand how Android applications interact with the operating system through application components, intents, permissions, and process lifecycle.
explanation: Android apps are packaged as APK or App Bundle artifacts and run inside an application process managed by Android. Activities represent user-facing entry points; services perform work without a UI; broadcast receivers respond to system or app events; and content providers expose structured data across process boundaries. Intents describe actions and carry data between components. The manifest declares components, capabilities, and permissions, while runtime permission flows are required for protected resources.
codeExample: // Launch another activity with an explicit intent\nval intent = Intent(this, DetailActivity::class.java)\nintent.putExtra("item_id", itemId)\nstartActivity(intent)
objective: Identify the purpose and lifecycle role of activities, services, receivers, and providers
objective: Choose explicit or implicit intents and pass data safely
objective: Declare components and request dangerous permissions at runtime
pitfall: Treating a background service as an unrestricted long-running process
pitfall: Assuming manifest permission declarations automatically grant runtime access
prerequisites: android-kotlin-language,android-studio-project
resource: Android Developers — Training Courses|https://developer.android.com/courses|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: android-architecture-patterns
title: Android App Architecture and Dependency Injection
roadmapId: android-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Separate UI, domain, and data responsibilities and manage dependencies so Android applications remain testable and maintainable.
explanation: A typical Android architecture separates UI state and rendering from business rules and data access. ViewModels expose observable UI state, repositories coordinate data sources, and domain use cases can isolate business operations when complexity warrants them. Dependency injection supplies implementations at defined boundaries and makes tests independent of real network or storage services. Architecture should remain proportional to app size; layers are useful when they clarify ownership rather than merely adding files.
codeExample: class ItemsViewModel(\n    private val repository: ItemsRepository\n) : ViewModel() {\n    val items = repository.observeItems()\n        .stateIn(viewModelScope, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), emptyList())\n}
objective: Assign UI, domain, and data responsibilities to appropriate components
objective: Use repositories to coordinate local and remote data sources
objective: Apply constructor injection and replace dependencies in tests
pitfall: Putting business rules directly in composables or activities
pitfall: Creating layers and abstractions without a clear ownership or testing benefit
prerequisites: android-compose-ui,android-persistence
resource: Android Developers — Training Courses|https://developer.android.com/courses|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: android-background-work
title: Background Work, Notifications, and WorkManager
roadmapId: android-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Select Android background execution APIs based on urgency, persistence, constraints, and user-visible behavior.
explanation: Android limits background execution to protect battery and responsiveness. WorkManager is suited to deferrable work that should survive process restarts and can declare constraints such as network availability. Foreground services are reserved for ongoing work that is noticeable to the user and must follow current platform restrictions. AlarmManager is for time-based events with specific timing needs. Notifications require channels on modern Android versions and should communicate useful, user-controlled events.
codeExample: val request = OneTimeWorkRequestBuilder<SyncWorker>()\n    .setConstraints(\n        Constraints.Builder()\n            .setRequiredNetworkType(NetworkType.CONNECTED)\n            .build()\n    ).build()\nWorkManager.getInstance(context).enqueue(request)
objective: Choose between WorkManager, foreground services, and alarms
objective: Define retry and backoff behavior for deferrable jobs
objective: Create notification channels and handle notification permission states
pitfall: Using a foreground service for work that can be deferred
pitfall: Assuming background jobs execute at an exact time
prerequisites: android-coroutines,android-platform-components
resource: Android Developers — Training Courses|https://developer.android.com/courses|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: android-performance-accessibility
title: Android Performance, Accessibility, and App Quality
roadmapId: android-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Profile Android apps, reduce startup and rendering costs, and make interfaces usable with assistive technologies.
explanation: Android performance work begins with measurement: inspect startup, frame timing, memory, network activity, and battery use with Android Studio profilers and platform tracing. Avoid blocking the main thread, unnecessary recomposition, excessive allocations, and oversized assets. Accessibility requires meaningful labels, logical focus order, adequate touch targets, scalable text, and testing with TalkBack and accessibility scanners. Quality checks should include multiple API levels, screen sizes, process recreation, and constrained network conditions.
codeExample: @Composable\nfun SaveButton(onSave: () -> Unit) {\n    Button(onClick = onSave) {\n        Text("Save")\n    }\n}
objective: Use profiling data to locate startup, rendering, and memory bottlenecks
objective: Apply accessibility semantics and test navigation with TalkBack
objective: Build a test matrix for API levels, screen sizes, and lifecycle events
pitfall: Optimizing based only on intuition instead of measurements
pitfall: Using decorative icons without accessible labels or descriptions
prerequisites: android-compose-ui,android-testing-release
resource: Android Developers — Training Courses|https://developer.android.com/courses|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: ios-apple-platform-foundations
title: Apple Platform Architecture, App Lifecycle, and Capabilities
roadmapId: ios-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 55
summary: Understand the iOS application model, app and scene lifecycle, entitlements, permissions, and platform services.
explanation: iOS apps are built as signed bundles with declared capabilities and entitlements. UIKit and SwiftUI applications participate in lifecycle events that affect foreground activity, state preservation, and resource use. Apps should respond to scene transitions, interruptions, and termination without assuming they remain active indefinitely. Protected resources such as camera, location, and contacts require purpose strings and user authorization. Platform capabilities should be requested only when the feature needs them.
codeExample: // SwiftUI scene lifecycle\n@Environment(\.scenePhase) private var scenePhase\n\n.onChange(of: scenePhase) { _, phase in\n    if phase == .background {\n        savePendingState()\n    }\n}
objective: Explain app and scene lifecycle transitions
objective: Configure bundle capabilities and entitlements for required features
objective: Request protected-resource authorization with clear purpose strings
pitfall: Assuming an app continues running after it enters the background
pitfall: Requesting permissions before explaining the feature that needs them
prerequisites: ios-swift-language,ios-xcode-project
resource: PostgreSQL Documentation — Tutorial|https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html|tutorial
resource: CMU Database Systems|https://15445.courses.cs.cmu.edu/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: ios-application-architecture
title: iOS Application Architecture and Dependency Management
roadmapId: ios-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Organize SwiftUI or UIKit applications around clear state ownership, feature boundaries, and testable dependencies.
explanation: SwiftUI views should describe presentation from state rather than own long-lived business logic. Observable models coordinate feature state, while repositories and service types isolate persistence and network access. Dependency injection can use initializers, environment values, or composition roots depending on scope. For larger apps, feature modules and explicit interfaces reduce coupling. Avoid adopting a single architecture pattern mechanically; select boundaries that make data flow and testing clear.
codeExample: protocol ProfileService {\n    func loadProfile() async throws -> Profile\n}\n\n@MainActor\nfinal class ProfileModel: ObservableObject {\n    @Published private(set) var profile: Profile?\n    private let service: ProfileService\n\n    init(service: ProfileService) { self.service = service }\n}
objective: Define ownership of view state and shared application state
objective: Separate services and repositories from presentation code
objective: Inject dependencies to test features without live services
pitfall: Putting network and persistence operations directly inside view bodies
pitfall: Using global mutable state as a substitute for explicit dependency ownership
prerequisites: ios-swiftui,ios-persistence-networking
resource: The Swift Programming Language|https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: ios-background-notifications
title: Background Execution, Push Notifications, and Deep Links
roadmapId: ios-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Implement background refresh and notification flows while respecting iOS execution limits and user privacy.
explanation: iOS grants limited background execution based on declared modes and system scheduling. BackgroundTasks supports refresh and processing tasks that the system schedules; it does not promise exact execution times. APNs delivers remote notifications, while user notification authorization controls visible alerts and sounds. Universal links and custom URL schemes route users into relevant app content. Notification payloads should be treated as untrusted input and validated before navigation or data access.
codeExample: import BackgroundTasks\n\nBGTaskScheduler.shared.register(\n    forTaskWithIdentifier: "com.example.refresh",\n    using: nil\n) { task in\n    scheduleRefreshWork(task as! BGAppRefreshTask)\n}
objective: Choose an appropriate background task type and handle expiration
objective: Request notification authorization and register with APNs
objective: Validate deep-link destinations and notification payload data
pitfall: Assuming background tasks run at a fixed interval
pitfall: Trusting identifiers or parameters received from a notification without validation
prerequisites: ios-concurrency,ios-xcode-project

=== TOPIC ===
id: ios-accessibility-performance
title: iOS Accessibility, Performance, and App Review Readiness
roadmapId: ios-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 65
summary: Improve app responsiveness and accessibility and prepare builds for reliable distribution.
explanation: Use Instruments to measure launch time, hangs, memory, energy, and network behavior. Reduce main-thread work, avoid unnecessary view updates, and manage image and collection memory. Accessibility includes VoiceOver labels and hints, Dynamic Type, sufficient contrast, reduced motion support, and logical focus order. Before release, verify privacy manifests and required-reason APIs where applicable, test permission denial and data deletion flows, and review App Store metadata and signing configuration.
codeExample: Text("Continue")\n    .accessibilityLabel("Continue to checkout")\n    .accessibilityHint("Opens the payment review screen")
objective: Use Instruments to identify measurable performance problems
objective: Support VoiceOver, Dynamic Type, and system accessibility settings
objective: Check privacy disclosures, signing, and release configuration
pitfall: Testing only on a simulator and overlooking device-specific performance
pitfall: Treating accessibility labels as a substitute for a usable focus and interaction model
prerequisites: ios-swiftui,ios-testing,ios-release-privacy
