=== TOPIC ===
id: game-math-rendering-foundations
title: Game Mathematics, Coordinate Spaces, and Rendering Pipeline
roadmapId: game-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Use vectors, transforms, coordinate spaces, and rendering concepts to reason about game objects and camera behavior.
explanation: Game code commonly uses vectors for position, direction, velocity, and forces. Transform hierarchies combine local and world coordinates; camera projection maps 3D scenes to the viewport. The rendering pipeline transforms vertices, assembles primitives, applies shaders, and writes pixels through depth and blending tests. A developer should understand the distinction between simulation state and rendered output before debugging movement, camera, or visual artifacts.
codeExample: # Godot-style vector movement\nvar direction := Vector3(1, 0, 0).normalized()\nvelocity = direction * move_speed
objective: Perform vector addition, scaling, normalization, and dot-product reasoning
objective: Convert between local and world coordinate spaces
objective: Describe the major stages from vertex data to rendered pixels
pitfall: Mixing local-space and world-space values
pitfall: Using frame-dependent movement without accounting for delta time
prerequisites: game-programming-basics,game-engine-workflow
resource: Godot — Step by Step|https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/getting_started/step_by_step/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: game-audio-save-data
title: Game Audio, Persistence, and Player Settings
roadmapId: game-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Build audio feedback and persistent player data for settings, progress, and save states.
explanation: Audio systems mix music, ambience, dialogue, and effects with separate buses or mixer groups. Audio should respond to gameplay events without creating duplicate or unbounded playback. Save systems serialize stable game state such as checkpoints, inventory, settings, and progression. Save formats need versioning and validation because game updates can change fields. Avoid serializing transient runtime objects or trusting editable save data for security-sensitive decisions.
codeExample: # Save a small settings object in Godot\nvar settings = {"master_volume": 0.8, "subtitles": true}\nvar file = FileAccess.open("user://settings.json", FileAccess.WRITE)\nfile.store_string(JSON.stringify(settings))
objective: Route audio categories through controllable buses
objective: Choose stable data to persist and define save checkpoints
objective: Version save data and handle missing or invalid fields
pitfall: Saving engine object references as if they were portable data
pitfall: Overwriting a valid save before confirming the new data was written
prerequisites: game-engine-workflow,game-assets-pipeline
resource: Godot — Step by Step|https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/getting_started/step_by_step/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: game-networked-multiplayer
title: Networked Multiplayer and Client-Server Game State
roadmapId: game-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 85
summary: Understand latency, authority, synchronization, and validation when building multiplayer gameplay.
explanation: Networked games must account for latency, jitter, packet loss, and clients that may send invalid actions. Authoritative servers validate important state changes and replicate selected state to clients. Snapshot interpolation smooths remote movement; client-side prediction can reduce perceived input delay but requires reconciliation when server state differs. Reliable and unreliable message delivery have different costs and should be selected based on whether an event must arrive and in what order.
codeExample: // Pseudocode: client sends an input command, server validates it\nsend_input(sequence_id, movement_vector)\nserver:\n    if valid_input(movement_vector):\n        apply_movement(authoritative_player, movement_vector)
objective: Explain server authority and client prediction trade-offs
objective: Distinguish reliable events from frequent state snapshots
objective: Design validation and reconciliation for player input
pitfall: Trusting the client to determine authoritative health, currency, or inventory
pitfall: Using reliable delivery for every high-frequency movement update
prerequisites: game-programming-basics,game-physics-collision
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: game-design-content-tools
title: Level Design, Procedural Content, and Developer Tooling
roadmapId: game-developer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Build maintainable content workflows and use procedural techniques where they improve replayability or production efficiency.
explanation: Game content should be authored with repeatable rules for scale, naming, collision, navigation, lighting, and asset budgets. Level design coordinates player goals, pacing, spatial readability, and encounter flow. Procedural generation can create layouts, variations, or item distributions, but generated content needs constraints and validation so it remains playable. Editor tools and import automation reduce repetitive work and make content changes easier to review.
codeExample: # Deterministic random generation\nvar rng := RandomNumberGenerator.new()\nrng.seed = level_seed\nvar room_variant := rng.randi_range(0, 3)
objective: Define asset and level conventions that support collaboration
objective: Use seeded randomness for reproducible procedural generation
objective: Validate generated levels for reachability and gameplay constraints
pitfall: Using random generation without bounds or playability checks
pitfall: Allowing asset import settings to vary unpredictably across contributors
prerequisites: game-engine-workflow,game-assets-pipeline
resource: Godot — Step by Step|https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/getting_started/step_by_step/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-electronics-digital-logic
title: Digital Electronics, Logic Levels, and Hardware Interfaces
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Read basic digital circuit behavior and connect firmware assumptions to real electrical interfaces.
explanation: Firmware interacts with physical signals. Digital inputs and outputs have voltage thresholds, current limits, pull-up or pull-down requirements, and timing constraints. Datasheets define pin multiplexing, absolute maximum ratings, input thresholds, and drive strength. Engineers should distinguish logic-level compatibility from protocol compatibility and use external level shifting or protection when required. Basic knowledge of resistors, switches, LEDs, debouncing, and transistor drivers helps prevent hardware damage.
codeExample: // Configure a GPIO output using a vendor HAL\ngpio_config_t cfg = {\n    .pin = LED_PIN,\n    .mode = GPIO_MODE_OUTPUT\n};\ngpio_configure(&cfg);\ngpio_write(LED_PIN, 1);
objective: Interpret pin voltage, current, and logic-level specifications
objective: Identify pull-up, pull-down, and switch-debounce requirements
objective: Check electrical compatibility before connecting peripherals
pitfall: Connecting pins based only on matching signal names
pitfall: Ignoring absolute maximum ratings and current limits
prerequisites: emb-mcu-architecture
resource: Pro Git Book|https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2|book
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-toolchain-startup-linker
title: Cross-Compilation, Linker Scripts, and MCU Startup
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Understand how embedded firmware is compiled, linked, placed in memory, and started after reset.
explanation: Embedded toolchains compile for a target architecture that may differ from the development host. The linker script maps code and data sections into flash and RAM. Startup code establishes the stack, copies initialized data, clears zero-initialized data, and transfers control to the runtime or main function. Vector tables define exception and interrupt entry points. Inspecting the map file and ELF sections helps diagnose memory overflow and unexpected placement.
codeExample: /* Illustrative linker memory regions */\nMEMORY {\n  FLASH (rx) : ORIGIN = 0x08000000, LENGTH = 256K\n  RAM   (rwx): ORIGIN = 0x20000000, LENGTH = 64K\n}
objective: Trace the cross-compilation and linking stages for a target MCU
objective: Explain vector tables and reset startup responsibilities
objective: Inspect map files and section sizes to verify memory placement
pitfall: Assuming desktop startup and runtime behavior applies unchanged to bare metal
pitfall: Ignoring linker output until firmware exceeds available flash or RAM
prerequisites: emb-c-firmware,emb-mcu-architecture
resource: Beej's Guide to C Programming|https://beej.us/guide/bgc/|tutorial
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-registers-volatile-dma
title: Memory-Mapped Registers, Volatile Access, and DMA
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Access hardware registers correctly and coordinate CPU and peripheral data movement.
explanation: Peripherals are controlled through memory-mapped registers whose fields have hardware-defined meanings. Volatile-qualified accesses prevent the compiler from removing or merging certain observable reads and writes, but volatile alone does not provide atomicity, mutual exclusion, or cache coherency. DMA transfers data between peripherals and memory with limited CPU involvement. Firmware must follow alignment, buffer lifetime, cache maintenance, and completion-interrupt requirements for the target MCU.
codeExample: #include <stdint.h>\n#define GPIO_OUT (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020014u)\n\nvoid set_output_bit(void) {\n    GPIO_OUT |= (1u << 5);\n}
objective: Read register descriptions and apply masks to individual fields
objective: Explain what volatile guarantees and what it does not guarantee
objective: Manage DMA buffers, completion events, and cache coherency requirements
pitfall: Using read-modify-write on registers with write-one-to-clear semantics
pitfall: Assuming volatile makes shared data safe across interrupts and tasks
prerequisites: emb-c-firmware,emb-peripherals-buses
resource: Making Embedded Systems — Resources|https://interrupt.memfault.com/blog/|articles
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-protocols-drivers
title: Peripheral Driver Design and Communication Protocol Debugging
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 70
summary: Build reusable drivers and debug timing and framing issues across common hardware protocols.
explanation: Drivers should separate device-specific register operations from higher-level application logic. UART uses framed serial data, SPI uses clocked master/slave transfers and chip select, and I²C uses addressed shared-bus transactions. CAN supports message-based arbitration and error handling in noisy distributed systems. A driver must define timeouts, error states, buffer ownership, and concurrency rules. Logic analyzers and oscilloscopes help compare actual waveforms with protocol expectations.
codeExample: // Driver interface separates application from hardware details\ntypedef struct {\n    int (*read)(uint8_t address, uint8_t *data, size_t length);\n    int (*write)(uint8_t address, const uint8_t *data, size_t length);\n} i2c_bus_t;
objective: Compare UART, SPI, I²C, and CAN communication characteristics
objective: Define driver interfaces with explicit timeout and error behavior
objective: Use logic-analyzer traces to diagnose protocol faults
pitfall: Assuming a successful API return means the peripheral accepted the command
pitfall: Failing to define bus ownership when multiple tasks share a peripheral
prerequisites: emb-peripherals-buses,emb-rtos-scheduling
resource: Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach — Resources|https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/index.php|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-safety-verification
title: Firmware Verification, Fault Handling, and Functional Safety Basics
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 85
summary: Plan verification and safe failure behavior for firmware that interacts with physical systems.
explanation: Embedded verification combines unit tests for pure logic, hardware abstraction mocks, integration tests on target boards, and hardware-in-the-loop tests. Fault handling should define behavior for sensor range violations, communication timeouts, watchdog resets, and brownouts. Safety-related projects require hazard analysis, traceable requirements, independent verification, and standards appropriate to the industry; a generic test suite does not establish certification. Static analysis and coding rules help prevent undefined behavior and resource misuse.
codeExample: // Example: reject out-of-range sensor readings\nbool sensor_value_valid(int value) {\n    return value >= SENSOR_MIN && value <= SENSOR_MAX;\n}
objective: Select test levels for firmware logic, peripherals, and complete hardware
objective: Define safe responses to timeout, invalid sensor data, and reset conditions
objective: Explain why safety standards and certification depend on application domain
pitfall: Treating watchdog resets as a substitute for fault diagnosis
pitfall: Claiming functional-safety compliance without the required process and evidence
prerequisites: emb-debug-testing,emb-power-security
resource: Making Embedded Systems — Resources|https://interrupt.memfault.com/blog/|articles
=== TOPIC ===
id: embedded-boot-update-diagnostics
title: Bootloaders, Firmware Updates, and Field Diagnostics
roadmapId: embedded-systems-engineer
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Design firmware update and diagnostic mechanisms that remain recoverable under power loss and device faults.
explanation: A bootloader selects and validates firmware images before transferring execution. Robust update schemes may use A/B slots, staged activation, image signatures, version checks, and rollback after failed startup. Diagnostics should expose useful device state without leaking secrets or destabilizing real-time behavior. Field logs, crash records, reset reasons, and update status help support deployed devices with limited physical access.
codeExample: // Conceptual image verification before activation\nif (verify_signature(candidate_image) &&\n    candidate_image.version >= minimum_allowed_version) {\n    mark_image_pending(candidate_image);\n}
objective: Describe bootloader responsibilities and image validation
objective: Compare single-slot, A/B, and rollback-capable update strategies
objective: Plan field diagnostics around reset causes and bounded log storage
pitfall: Replacing the only working image before verifying the candidate
pitfall: Allowing unsigned or downgraded firmware to bypass device policy
prerequisites: embedded-toolchain-startup-linker,emb-power-security
resource: Making Embedded Systems — Resources|https://interrupt.memfault.com/blog/|articles
=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-system-context-stakeholders
title: System Context, Stakeholders, and Architectural Drivers
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Beginner
estimatedMinutes: 60
summary: Define system boundaries, stakeholders, constraints, and the drivers that shape architecture.
explanation: Architecture begins by identifying users, external systems, business capabilities, regulatory constraints, and operational context. Functional requirements describe behavior; quality attribute scenarios describe measurable conditions such as latency, availability, security, and modifiability. Constraints include existing platforms, team skills, delivery deadlines, and data residency. An architect records assumptions and unresolved risks so design choices can be reviewed against real needs.
codeExample: # Quality attribute scenario (example)\nStimulus: 500 concurrent users submit a search\nResponse: the service returns results\nMeasure: p95 latency remains below 300 ms
objective: Map system actors and external dependencies
objective: Turn vague quality goals into measurable scenarios
objective: Record constraints, assumptions, and architectural risks
pitfall: Treating “scalable” or “secure” as measurable requirements without scenarios
pitfall: Starting component design before agreeing on system boundaries
prerequisites: arch-quality-attributes
resource: C4 Model — Software Architecture|https://c4model.com/|guide
=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-system-design-distributed
title: Distributed Systems Design and Service Decomposition
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 90
summary: Design service boundaries and reason about consistency, communication, failure, and deployment trade-offs.
explanation: Distributed systems introduce partial failure, network latency, duplicated messages, independent deployment, and data ownership questions. Decomposition should follow business capabilities and change boundaries rather than splitting every entity into a service. Architects must choose synchronous or asynchronous communication, define timeouts and retry policies, and understand consistency guarantees. A modular monolith can be a better starting point when independent scaling or deployment does not justify distributed complexity.
codeExample: # Idempotency key prevents duplicate command effects\nPOST /payments\nIdempotency-Key: 8f3a...\n
objective: Identify when a modular monolith or service decomposition is appropriate
objective: Choose communication patterns based on latency and coupling
objective: Explain consistency, idempotency, and partial-failure behavior
pitfall: Creating microservices around database tables rather than business capabilities
pitfall: Retrying non-idempotent operations without deduplication
prerequisites: arch-styles-patterns,arch-api-contracts

=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-data-architecture-integration
title: Data Architecture, Integration, and Ownership
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Define data ownership, integration contracts, and consistency boundaries across application components.
explanation: Data architecture determines authoritative sources, write ownership, read models, retention, and access patterns. Shared databases can simplify transactions but increase coupling between independently changing components. Separate data stores improve ownership but require explicit synchronization and reconciliation. Event-driven integration needs schema evolution, delivery semantics, replay strategy, and handling for duplicate or out-of-order events. Data classification and lifecycle policies should be part of the design.
codeExample: {\n  "event": "OrderPlaced",\n  "version": 1,\n  "orderId": "ord_123",\n  "occurredAt": "2026-01-01T10:00:00Z"\n}
objective: Assign authoritative ownership for business data
objective: Compare shared-database and service-owned-data trade-offs
objective: Design event schemas and consumers for evolution and duplicate delivery
pitfall: Allowing multiple services to update the same data without an ownership rule
pitfall: Assuming event delivery is exactly once across a distributed system
prerequisites: arch-api-contracts,arch-domain-boundaries
resource: C4 Model — Software Architecture|https://c4model.com/|guide
=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-cloud-deployment-evolution
title: Deployment Topologies, Scalability, and Cost Trade-offs
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 80
summary: Choose deployment and scaling approaches that fit workload, reliability, team, and cost constraints.
explanation: Architecture decisions include process boundaries, runtime platforms, regions, availability zones, scaling dimensions, and dependency placement. Horizontal scaling requires stateless request handling or externalized state; stateful components need partitioning, replication, or carefully managed capacity. Multi-region designs add data consistency and operational complexity. Cost should be modeled alongside latency and availability, including network transfer, storage growth, observability, and idle capacity.
codeExample: # Example deployment decision record\nWorkload: stateless HTTP API\nChoice: containerized service with horizontal autoscaling\nConstraint: database connection pool is the scaling bottleneck
objective: Compare vertical, horizontal, and partition-based scaling
objective: Evaluate single-region and multi-region deployment costs
objective: Identify resource and dependency bottlenecks before selecting topology
pitfall: Adding regions without defining data consistency and failover behavior
pitfall: Optimizing infrastructure cost while ignoring operational ownership
prerequisites: arch-system-design-distributed,arch-quality-attributes
resource: Nand2Tetris|https://www.nand2tetris.org/|course
=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-security-threat-modeling
title: Architecture Security, Privacy, and Threat Modeling
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Intermediate
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Build security and privacy into system boundaries, identity flows, data handling, and operational controls.
explanation: Architectural security starts with assets, trust boundaries, entry points, actors, and abuse cases. Threat modeling methods such as STRIDE help identify spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, and privilege escalation. Controls include identity and access management, encryption, key management, audit trails, secure defaults, and data minimization. Privacy design also considers purpose limitation, retention, deletion, and jurisdictional requirements.
codeExample: # Example trust-boundary checklist\n- Authenticate every external caller\n- Authorize each operation against resource ownership\n- Validate untrusted input at the boundary\n- Record security-relevant actions
objective: Map trust boundaries and sensitive assets
objective: Apply a threat-modeling method to components and data flows
objective: Select controls for identity, secrets, encryption, audit, and retention
pitfall: Treating network location as proof of identity
pitfall: Adding encryption without defining key ownership and rotation
prerequisites: arch-quality-attributes,arch-domain-boundaries
resource: OWASP Web Security Testing Guide|https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/|documentation
=== TOPIC ===
id: arch-governance-fitness-functions
title: Architecture Governance, Fitness Functions, and Technical Evolution
roadmapId: software-architect
type: standard
targetSubroadmap: 
difficulty: Advanced
estimatedMinutes: 75
summary: Keep architecture aligned with changing requirements through lightweight governance and automated checks.
explanation: Architecture governance defines who makes decisions, how exceptions are reviewed, and which constraints must be maintained. Fitness functions turn architectural rules into repeatable checks, such as dependency direction, API compatibility, performance budgets, security policies, and deployment limits. Architecture reviews should focus on consequential trade-offs and evidence rather than approval ceremonies. Evolution planning includes migration paths, deprecation policies, technical debt ownership, and feedback from production operations.
codeExample: # Example architectural rule (conceptual)\nassert no_dependency(\n    source_layer="domain",\n    target_layer="infrastructure"\n)
objective: Define decision rights and an architecture exception process
objective: Translate important constraints into automated fitness functions
objective: Plan migrations and deprecations with measurable exit criteria
pitfall: Using governance as a substitute for engineering feedback
pitfall: Writing architecture rules that cannot be tested or tied to a real risk
prerequisites: arch-adrs-diagrams,arch-system-design-distributed
resource: C4 Model — Software Architecture|https://c4model.com/|guide
